From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0360-lichii-cpu-0230-inline-cntpct-read.patch

Source: 0360-lichii-cpu-0230-inline-cntpct-read.patch
Core patch. Paths are the submodule's. Applies on core16 alone or in the lichii-cpu order.

Source: zortos293/Lichii PR 161, commit d51b04d80a, GPL-3.0-or-later. Rewritten for this core. PR 161
was still open on 6 Nov.

EDEN_NX2_INLINE_CNTPCT=1: MRS CNTPCT_EL0 becomes RDTSC and one multiply in the block, instead of a
host call into CoreTiming::GetClockTicks and WallClock::GetCNTPCT. Unset, the code is as before.
EDEN_NX2_CNTPCT_PAUSE=0 still turns the polling pause off.

Changed from Lichii:
- PR 161 bakes &m_cb->m_last_cntpct into the code. With our shared JIT (headless/dynarmic/
  jit_group.h) any core runs code another core compiled, so one core's field would be read and
  written by all of them. The last read lives in A64JitState and is reached through the JIT state
  register, the way shared-jit.cmake moved TPIDR and the monitor slots.
- No base_rdtsc: this core's GetUptime is the raw counter, so the base is 0 and guest values are
  the same as the callback's. WallClock::GuestCNTPCTFactor gives the factor.
- Not the skip of the pause on non-SMT hosts: the console has SMT, and that test would be false.
- Default off here.

Same guard as PR 161: a wall clock, CpuClock::Normal, no sync_core_speed. WallClock::IsNative is
replaced by the factor being 0 when the counter is not invariant.

Console figures: EDEN_PERF_CLOCK says invariant=1 tsc_hz=1596300000 native=1, and
EDEN_PERF_CLOCK_READ gives 26 ns a read for lfence, rdtsc, lfence and the multiply.

Risk: the fences go and nothing replaces them. RDTSC can run ahead of earlier loads and stores, so a
guest that stamps on one core and reads the clock on another can see time step back. How far is a
guess: a few ticks of 32 ns when a cache miss is in the way. ISB does not help: on x64 it ends the
block and emits no fence (hook_isb is off). PR 161 has the same gap.

Pool memory: none. 8 bytes per core.

Checked on the Mac: headless cmake anchors pass (host and PS5 mode), and dynarmic built for x86-64
with the frontend's derived sources ran under Rosetta: callback path unchanged, value equals
high(tsc * factor) between two host reads, 26 live guest registers survive, polling loop ends,
four JITs of one group run the same blocks at once. Not run on the console.

diff --git a/src/common/cpu_features.cpp b/src/common/cpu_features.cpp
index c7f6e6f036..c304281f7a 100644
--- a/src/common/cpu_features.cpp
+++ b/src/common/cpu_features.cpp
@@ -438,6 +438,14 @@ u64 WallClock::NsToTicks(std::chrono::nanoseconds ns) const {
 }
 #endif
 
+u64 WallClock::GuestCNTPCTFactor() const {
+#if defined(ARCHITECTURE_x86_64)
+    return invariant ? cntpct_rdtsc_factor.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) : 0;
+#else
+    return 0;
+#endif
+}
+
 u64 WallClock::CPUTickToGuestCNTPCT(u64 cpu_tick) const {
     return MultiplyAndDivide64(cpu_tick, GetGuestCNTFRQ(), CPUTickFreq);
 }
